    <body>
      <sec id="article-26809.s1" sec-type="Continuing Education Activity">
        <title>Continuing Education Activity</title>
        <p>Obstructive sleep apnea (OSA) is a significant but frequently underrecognized disorder in pediatrics, particularly among children with neurodevelopmental delay. The American Thoracic Society and the American Academy of Pediatrics define pediatric OSA as a sleep-related breathing disorder marked by recurrent partial or complete upper airway obstruction leading to disrupted ventilation and fragmented sleep.</p>
        <p>The condition arises from anatomical, neuromuscular, and inflammatory factors. Adenotonsillar hypertrophy represents the predominant etiology in younger children, whereas obesity is the leading cause in older children and adolescents. Risk factors include craniofacial anomalies, allergic rhinitis, neuromuscular weakness, and exposure to secondhand smoke. Snoring, restless sleep, behavioral changes, and daytime somnolence are common manifestations.</p>
        <p>Diagnosis&#x000a0;hinges on clinical suspicion and is confirmed by overnight polysomnography, which quantifies apnea&#x02013;hypopnea events. First-line management includes adenotonsillectomy, while weight reduction and treatment of comorbidities are recommended adjuncts. Continuous positive airway pressure therapy may be required for residual disease. Untreated OSA predisposes to cardiovascular, neurocognitive, and growth complications. Prognosis is generally favorable with early recognition and appropriate intervention.</p>
        <p>This activity for healthcare professionals is designed to strengthen&#x000a0;learners'&#x000a0;proficiency in evaluating and managing pediatric OSA. Participants will advance their mastery of the condition's etiology, risk factors, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, and evidence-based diagnostic and therapeutic approaches. Improved skills will equip clinicians to collaborate with interprofessional teams providing care for affected children.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Objectives:</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Identify the clinical and diagnostic findings indicative of pediatric obstructive sleep apnea,&#x000a0;paying attention to etiologic and modifiable risk factors to guide management.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Apply evidence-based, individualized strategies for managing&#x000a0;pediatric obstructive sleep apnea and mitigating its potential complications.&#x000a0;</p></list-item><list-item><p>Improve&#x000a0;parental&#x000a0;and caregiver awareness of the causes, symptoms, and consequences of pediatric obstructive sleep apnea to promote early recognition, adherence to treatment, and healthier sleep practices.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Collaborate with all members of the interprofessional team, including specialists such as otolaryngologists, pediatricians, and sleep physicians, to provide efficient, comprehensive, and coordinated care for children with&#x000a0;obstructive sleep apnea.</p></list-item></list>
<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/account/trialuserreg/?articleid=26809&#x00026;utm_source=pubmed&#x00026;utm_campaign=reviews&#x00026;utm_content=26809">Access free multiple choice questions on this topic.</ext-link>
</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-26809.s2" sec-type="pubmed-excerpt">
        <title>Introduction</title>
        <p>Sleep is an active physiologic and neurologic process essential for multiple restorative functions. Adequate quantity and quality of sleep exert a significant influence on memory consolidation, cognitive performance, learning capacity, somatic growth, and cardiovascular health in children. Sleep-disordered breathing (SDB) encompasses a spectrum of disorders characterized by increased upper airway resistance and pharyngeal collapsibility, ranging from primary snoring to obstructive sleep apnea (OSA), which may occur across all age groups.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26809.r1">[1]</xref></p>
        <p>Pediatric OSA is a sleep-related breathing disorder characterized by recurrent episodes of partial or complete upper airway obstruction during sleep, resulting in intermittent hypoxemia, hypercapnia, and sleep fragmentation. The condition adversely affects behavior, neurocognitive development, metabolism, and overall health in children. Early recognition, thorough evaluation, and appropriate management are critical to preventing long-term sequelae.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26809.r2">[2]</xref> This activity&#x000a0;discusses the etiology, clinical manifestations, diagnostic approach, prognosis, and management of OSA in children. The discussion of adult OSA is presented separately.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26809.r3">[3]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-26809.s3" sec-type="Etiology">
        <title>Etiology</title>
        <p>Sleep apnea is classified into&#x000a0;2 primary categories based on etiology: central and obstructive.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26809.r4">[4]</xref> Central sleep apnea results from pathology within the central nervous system, characterized by impaired neurologically mediated respiratory drive without associated respiratory effort. OSA, which accounts for approximately 95% of diagnosed sleep apnea cases, results from the complete or partial collapse of the upper airway during sleep, leading to arousals or oxygen desaturation of 3% or greater.</p>
        <p>Any factor that reduces airway diameter or structural integrity may contribute to OSA, including anatomic, physiologic, genetic, environmental, and neuromuscular influences. Intrinsic and extrinsic mechanisms increase upper airway collapse risk. Intrinsic factors involve the critical closing pressure required to maintain airway patency, whereas extrinsic factors include fat deposition, tissue hypertrophy, and craniofacial abnormalities that alter normal anatomy. Additional risk factors associated with pediatric OSA include male sex, history of prematurity, daycare attendance, lower socioeconomic status, and African American race (see <bold>Images</bold>.&#x000a0;Etiology of Pediatric Obstructive Sleep Apnea;&#x000a0;Anatomical Differences in Pediatric Airway Related to Sleep Apnea).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26809.r5">[5]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26809.r6">[6]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-26809.s4" sec-type="Epidemiology">
        <title>Epidemiology</title>
        <p>The incidence of pediatric OSA peaks between ages 2 and 8 years, coinciding with increased tonsillar and adenoidal growth relative to upper airway dimensions in this developmental period. The prevalence of OSA in children is estimated at 2% to 5%, though higher rates are observed in certain clinical populations. Adenotonsillar hypertrophy characterizes the classical form, which predominates in children aged 3 to 5 years. Obesity-related OSA, resembling the adult phenotype, is more prevalent in adolescents. Morphologic, structural, and congenital variants occur in genetic syndromes such as Down syndrome, Prader-Willi syndrome, achondroplasia, and Pierre Robin sequence. Neuromuscular and lysosomal storage disorders also confer increased susceptibility across pediatric age groups.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26809.r7">[7]</xref></p>
        <p>In a study by Lumeng et al, habitual snoring was reported in 1.5% to 6% of children, apneic events in 0.2% to 4%, parent-reported SDB in 4% to 11%, and clinically diagnosed OSA in 1% to 4%. SDB was found to be more common in boys, children with higher body weight, and African American populations. The study identified insufficient age-specific prevalence data and recommended further epidemiologic investigation to better define pediatric SDB patterns.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26809.r8">[8]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-26809.s5" sec-type="Pathophysiology">
        <title>Pathophysiology</title>
        <p>Diagnosis of OSA is established by quantifying apneic and hypopneic events during sleep. Specific diagnostic criteria are discussed in the <bold>Evaluation</bold> section.</p>
        <p>Four principal factors contribute to the development of OSA: obesity, lymphoid hyperplasia, craniofacial abnormalities, and neuromuscular dysfunction. Each of these mechanisms increases extrinsic or intrinsic forces that compromise upper airway patency.</p>
        <p>Obesity results in peripharyngeal fat deposition, which elevates extrinsic pressure and promotes airway collapse during sleep. Lymphoid hyperplasia, particularly tonsillar and adenoidal enlargement, narrows the airway lumen.&#x000a0;Muscle relaxation during sleep exacerbates obstruction.</p>
        <p>Neuromuscular dysfunction contributes to OSA through impaired airway tone and control. This&#x000a0;pathophysiologic mechanism is frequently observed in conditions such as Down syndrome, where hypotonia increases airway collapsibility.&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Craniofacial abnormalities further predispose to OSA. Syndromes such as Crouzon, Pierre Robin, and Apert, as well as cleft lip and palate, alter airway geometry and reduce posterior oropharyngeal space. Features including micrognathia, macroglossia, and midface hypoplasia contribute to structural narrowing and elevate the risk of upper airway obstruction during sleep.</p>
        <p>Katz et al (2007) reviewed the pathophysiology of pediatric OSA, emphasizing the influence of craniofacial and soft tissue structures on upper airway dimensions. Narrowing and retropositioning of the maxilla and mandible predispose individuals to airway obstruction. Neuromuscular activation, arousal threshold, and ventilatory control further determine upper airway stability, which depends on the tonic activity of pharyngeal dilator muscles during wakefulness. Activation of these muscles decreases markedly during sleep. In rapid eye movement (REM) sleep, diminished pharyngeal dilator activity results from central REM-related processes.&#x000a0;During nonrapid eye movement (NREM) sleep, reduced ventilatory drive following ventilatory overshoot leads to transient decreases in airway muscle tone and consequent obstructive events.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26809.r9">[9]</xref></p>
        <p>Congenital syndromes associated with craniofacial abnormalities frequently contribute to pediatric OSA. Pierre Robin sequence, Crouzon syndrome, and Apert syndrome cause micrognathia and glossoptosis, resulting in airway narrowing. Macroglossia and neuromuscular dysfunction in Down syndrome similarly predispose to airway obstruction. Children with Prader-Willi syndrome exhibit hypotonia, increasing airway collapsibility, while Treacher Collins syndrome leads to obstruction through facial skeletal deformities. In mucopolysaccharidoses, glycosaminoglycan accumulation within soft tissues of the airway produces mechanical narrowing&#x000a0;(see <bold>Image</bold>. Pathologic Breathing Cycle&#x000a0;in Obstructive Sleep Apnea).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26809.r10">[10]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-26809.s6" sec-type="History and Physical">
        <title>History and Physical</title>
        <p>Evaluation of sleep quantity and quality is an essential component of pediatric assessment. Studies indicate considerable variation in sleep screening practices across healthcare settings. Although no universally adopted screening tool exists specifically for pediatric OSA, current guidelines recommend that clinicians assess sleep quantity, quality, and the presence of snoring at every well-child visit. When poor sleep quality is suspected, additional questions should address frequent awakenings, abnormal sleep positioning, and disrupted bed coverings, which may indicate excessive nighttime movements. Parental reports are particularly valuable, as caregivers may not volunteer concerns regarding sleep without specific prompting.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26809.r11">[11]</xref></p>
        <p>Craniofacial abnormalities, specific syndromes, and early-life events increase the risk of OSA. Repeated screening is advised for patients with neuromuscular disorders, Down syndrome, and craniofacial malformations. Children with a history of prematurity also demonstrate an elevated risk for OSA, even in the absence of other predisposing factors. Studies have shown that up to 9.4% of school-aged children born prematurely are later diagnosed with OSA. Since these patients may not display the classic features of pediatric OSA, a low threshold for referral for polysomnography (PSG) is warranted.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26809.r12">[12]</xref></p>
        <p>Children often exhibit clinical manifestations that differ from the classic adult presentation of OSA, with behavioral changes being the most prominent distinguishing feature. Parents frequently report snoring, mouth breathing, witnessed apneas, frequent nighttime awakenings, and secondary nocturnal enuresis. Disrupted sleep in pediatric patients can manifest as hyperactivity, irritability, or aggression, which often leads caregivers to seek medical evaluation.</p>
        <p>Various tools and questionnaires have been developed to assess symptoms of OSA in both adults and children. The adult STOP-BANG questionnaire has been modified for adolescents as the Teen STOP-BANG screening tool. In this version, the question regarding &#x0201c;age greater than 50 years&#x0201d; is replaced with one addressing academic difficulties, as learning problems are a recognized symptom of SDB in children. The questionnaire consists of&#x000a0;8 yes-or-no questions, with scoring interpreted as follows:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>0&#x000a0;to 2: Low risk</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>3&#x000a0;to 4: Intermediate risk</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>5&#x000a0;to 8: High risk</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Table 1&#x000a0;outlines the&#x000a0;adaptations incorporated in&#x000a0;the&#x000a0;Teen STOP-BANG questionnaire (see <bold>Table 1</bold>. Comparison of the Adult and Teen STOP-BANG Questionnaires).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26809.r13">[13]</xref></p>
        <p>The Epworth Sleepiness Scale has been adapted for pediatric use as the Epworth Sleepiness Scale for Children and Adolescents (ESS-CHAD). Janssen et al demonstrated in 2017 that the ESS-CHAD is a reliable and internally valid instrument for assessing daytime sleepiness in adolescents aged 12 to 18 years. Further studies were recommended to establish&#x000a0;the tool's internal validity in children younger than 12 years, as well as to confirm external validity and determine accurate cutoff values for both children and adolescents.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26809.r14">[14]</xref>&#x000a0;(Sources:&#x000a0;<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/neuroscience/daytime-somnolence">Guilleminault et al</ext-link>, 2011;&#x000a0;<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/nursing-and-health-professions/internal-validity#featured-authors">Kane et al</ext-link>, 2025)</p>
        <p>On physical examination, general appearance provides valuable diagnostic clues. Patients may appear fatigued or, conversely, exhibit hyperactivity. Findings may include allergic shiners, swollen nasal mucosa, micrognathia, macroglossia, high-arched palate, adenoidal facies, and tonsillar hypertrophy. Neurologic assessment should include evaluation of muscle tone, and cardiovascular examination should be performed to assess for pulmonary hypertension.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26809.r15">[15]</xref></p>
        <p>Hyponasal speech and nasal obstruction are frequently noted. Increasing rates of childhood obesity&#x000a0;are a growing risk factor for pediatric OSA. Height, weight, and body mass index (BMI) should be measured at every clinical encounter, as each 1 kg/m&#x000b2; increase in BMI above the 50th percentile raises the risk for OSA by approximately 12%.</p>
        <table-wrap id="article-26809.table0" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
