        </table-wrap>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-26809.s7" sec-type="Evaluation">
        <title>Evaluation</title>
        <p>Nocturnal PSG is the diagnostic gold standard when the history and physical examination suggest pediatric OSA. However, PSG is resource-intensive, time-consuming, and costly, limiting its availability in many settings. Overnight home oximetry can provide supportive information but does not replace PSG for definitive diagnosis. When an underlying cardiopulmonary disorder is suspected, chest radiography and electrocardiography are recommended. A cardiac evaluation, including echocardiography, is particularly important before surgical intervention in children with severe OSA.</p>
        <p>Emerging evidence indicates that specific inflammatory biomarkers, including kallikrein-1, uromodulin, urocortin-3, and orosomucoid-1, may be elevated in pediatric OSA and could enhance diagnostic precision. Routine laboratory evaluation may include a complete blood count, iron studies, and thyroid-stimulating hormone levels to identify alternative or contributing causes of sleep disturbance. Imaging studies can help delineate anatomic abnormalities predisposing to airway obstruction but should be interpreted in conjunction with PSG findings for diagnostic confirmation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26809.r16">[16]</xref></p>
        <p>Multiple physiologic parameters of sleep quality and quantity are recorded during PSG. Sensors measure electroencephalographic activity, heart rate, nasal and oral airflow, oxygen saturation, limb movements, ocular movements, and snoring intensity. These data are used to calculate sleep onset latency, sleep efficiency, and time spent in each sleep stage.</p>
        <p>For the diagnosis of OSA, PSG quantifies the apnea-hypopnea index (AHI)&#x02014;the average number of apneic and hypopneic episodes per hour of sleep. The interpretation of AHI differs between adults and children, with lower thresholds in pediatric populations representing more severe disease. AHI is the only standardized and scientifically accepted measure for grading OSA severity, providing an objective and reproducible metric. Table 2 presents the AHI grading criteria for assessing OSA severity in both pediatric and adult patients (see&#x000a0;<bold>Table 2.</bold> Apnea-Hypopnea Index Criteria for Grading the Severity of Obstructive Sleep Apnea in Children and Adults).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26809.r17">[17]</xref></p>
        <table-wrap id="article-26809.table1" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <title>Table 2. Apnea-Hypopnea Index Criteria for Grading the Severity of Obstructive Sleep Apnea in Children and Adults</title>
          </caption>
          <table style="width: 376.568px;">
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 107px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Obstructive Sleep Apnea Severity Grade</bold></p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 124px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Apnea-Hypopnea Index in Children (events per hour)</bold></p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 122.568px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Apnea-Hypopnea Index in Adults (events per hour)</bold></p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 107px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>None</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 124px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>0</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 122.568px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>&#x0003c; 5</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 107px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Mild</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 124px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>1 to 4.9</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 122.568px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>5 to 15</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 107px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Moderate</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 124px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>5 to 9.9</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 122.568px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>15 to 30</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 107px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Severe</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 124px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>&#x0003e; 10</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 122.568px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>&#x0003e; 30</p>
</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </table-wrap>
        <p>An AHI of 1 or greater is considered abnormal in children aged 13 years and younger.&#x000a0;However, the clinical significance of an AHI between 1 and 1.9 events per hour remains debated.</p>
        <p>For patients aged 13 to 17 years, either pediatric or adult criteria may be applied, depending on clinical context and developmental status. Since&#x000a0;children generally have greater upper airway integrity and lower airway collapsibility than adults, PSG findings are often dominated by hypopneic events rather than complete apneas. Additionally, hypercarbia may occur in pediatric OSA due to prolonged hypoventilation, a feature less commonly seen in adult populations.</p>
        <p>Pulse oximetry and home sleep apnea testing are alternative methods for monitoring SDB outside the laboratory setting. These approaches record fewer parameters than full PSG but may offer a more convenient and cost-effective option. Although&#x000a0;home sleep apnea testing is an acceptable diagnostic tool for uncomplicated adult OSA, the American Academy of Sleep Medicine (AASM) has found insufficient evidence to support its use in pediatric patients.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26809.r18">[18]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26809.r19">[19]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26809.r18">[18]</xref></p>
        <p>Several radiologic modalities are available for the evaluation of the upper airway in suspected pediatric OSA, including lateral neck radiographs, cephalometric analysis, fluoroscopy, computed tomography (CT), and magnetic resonance imaging (MRI). These imaging techniques are primarily used in research, as well as in the clinical assessment of children with craniofacial abnormalities or neurological disorders, to identify the level of airway obstruction and guide management planning. However, radiation exposure associated with x-rays and CT scans, along with the limitations of airway evaluation in the awake state, restricts their use in routine pediatric assessment.</p>
        <p>Dynamic MRI and drug-induced sleep endoscopy have emerged as valuable alternatives for upper airway evaluation in children. Drug-induced sleep endoscopy involves the use of a flexible endoscope to visualize airway dynamics while the child is in a pharmacologically induced sleep-like state, providing direct visualization of obstruction sites (see <bold>Image</bold>. Evaluation of Pediatric Obstructive Sleep Apnea).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26809.r20">[20]</xref></p>
        <p>The AASM Manual for the Scoring of Sleep and Associated Events, Version 3 includes several key updates and new sections. Additions encompass&#x000a0;the reporting of the highest and lowest heart rates during sleep, the central apnea index, and the duration below specified oxygen saturation thresholds. The updated version also recommends reporting the occurrence of snoring in children and introduces new sections addressing the multiple sleep latency test, maintenance of wakefulness test, and rules for scoring atrioventricular heart block, pacemaker rhythm, and arousals preceding transitions to wakefulness. (Source: <ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://aasm.org/wp-content/uploads/2023/02/Summary-of-Updates-v3.pdf">AASM</ext-link>, 2023)</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-26809.s8" sec-type="Treatment / Management">
        <title>Treatment / Management</title>
        <p>Treatment options for pediatric OSA vary according to disease severity and include both surgical and nonsurgical modalities. A therapeutic trial of leukotriene inhibitors, such as montelukast, may be considered in pediatric patients with mild-to-moderate OSA.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26809.r21">[21]</xref></p>
        <p>Montelukast has demonstrated efficacy in reducing adenotonsillar size after approximately&#x000a0;3 months of therapy, resulting in a corresponding decrease in AHI in appropriately selected patients.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26809.r22">[22]</xref> However, montelukast has been associated with serious neuropsychiatric adverse events, including suicidal ideation, depression, anxiety, agitation, and hallucinations. Since behavioral changes are also common in pediatric OSA, families and caregivers should be counseled to closely monitor for these symptoms and distinguish them from baseline behavioral manifestations. In March 2020, the U.S. Food and Drug Administration mandated a black box warning for montelukast due to its potential for serious mental health side effects.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26809.r23">[23]</xref></p>
        <p>Systemic glucocorticoids have not shown benefit in the management of pediatric OSA. In contrast, intranasal corticosteroid therapy administered for&#x000a0;6 weeks has been shown to improve AHI and may be considered for patients with mild-to-moderate disease. When used in combination with leukotriene inhibitors, intranasal corticosteroids have been shown to produce a clinically significant reduction in AHI in most patients.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26809.r24">[24]</xref></p>
        <p>If adenotonsillar hypertrophy is present, the primary and most effective treatment is adenotonsillectomy. This procedure is recommended for most pediatric patients with an AHI greater than 9 events per hour, as well as those with mild-to-moderate disease accompanied by significant symptoms.</p>
        <p>Partial tonsillectomy may be considered as an alternative approach, offering reduced postoperative complications and shorter recovery time. However, reported rates of tonsillar regrowth range from 7.2% to 16.6%. Other surgical procedures, including lingual tonsillectomy and uvulopalatopharyngoplasty, have limited evidence and have not demonstrated superiority over adenotonsillectomy. Although more conservative in scope, adenoidectomy alone has not shown superior outcomes compared with&#x000a0;adenotonsillectomy&#x000a0;in the management of pediatric OSA. The potential for recurrence of OSA and the need for repeat surgery following adenoidectomy should be carefully weighed in treatment planning.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26809.r25">[25]</xref></p>
        <p>A period of observation or &#x0201c;watchful waiting&#x0201d; for up to&#x000a0;6 months may be appropriate for patients with mild-to-moderate OSA. This approach allows for the management of underlying conditions such as obesity or allergic rhinitis, which may improve OSA and reduce AHI without surgical intervention. However, in pediatric patients&#x000a0;without obesity or underlying syndromic conditions, adenotonsillectomy has been shown to produce superior improvement in AHI.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26809.r26">[26]</xref></p>
        <p>Continuous positive airway pressure (CPAP) is another potential treatment option. While CPAP is considered 1st-line therapy in adults, its use in children presents several limitations. CPAP should be considered during the perioperative period before adenotonsillectomy for severe OSA if the child is not a suitable surgical candidate, or moderate-to-severe OSA persists following surgery. Compliance can be challenging in pediatric patients, and prolonged use of the same mask may alter facial structure. CPAP is particularly&#x000a0;indicated in&#x000a0;children&#x000a0;who have severe obesity, craniofacial abnormalities&#x000a0;that preclude&#x000a0;adenotonsillectomy, or residual&#x000a0;OSA postoperatively.</p>
        <p>High-flow nasal cannula therapy represents an alternative for children who&#x000a0;cannot tolerate CPAP. High-flow nasal cannula delivers heated, high-flow, humidified air through a nasal cannula, improving sleep quality and reducing respiratory events in children&#x000a0;with OSA.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26809.r27">[27]</xref></p>
        <p>The AASM issued a position statement regarding CPAP in pediatric OSA, noting that CPAP&#x000a0;may be safely and effectively used in the outpatient setting,&#x000a0;particularly for children of young age or lower body weight.&#x000a0;The statement emphasizes that clinicians with expertise in pediatric OSA should perform evaluation and management of CPAP therapy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26809.r28">[28]</xref></p>
        <p>Oral appliances have been shown to reduce AHI in adults.&#x000a0;However, insufficient studies exist in pediatric populations to determine the efficacy of these tools or identify which patients might benefit. Adjusting oral appliances to accommodate a child&#x02019;s growth would require frequent refittings. Rapid maxillary expansion may be considered in prepubescent patients and is particularly useful for&#x000a0;children&#x000a0;who have high-arched palates or do not have obesity but&#x000a0;have residual OSA following adenotonsillectomy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26809.r29">[29]</xref></p>
        <p>Myofunctional therapy is an emerging approach in the management of both pediatric and adult OSA. This therapy involves retraining the muscles of the oral cavity and oropharyngeal structures, as well as optimizing tongue posture. Data on efficacy in children are limited, with only small pediatric studies reported. Nevertheless, myofunctional therapy is a potential adjunctive treatment and a focus for future research.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26809.r30">[30]</xref></p>
        <p>Patients with reversible risk factors for OSA, particularly obesity, should receive counseling on risk factor modification. Weight loss can improve OSA and serve as an adjunctive therapy in older children. Caregivers should also be advised to minimize exposure to secondhand smoke, as environmental tobacco exposure has been associated with an increased risk of pediatric OSA.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26809.r31">[31]</xref></p>
        <p>Other upper airway surgeries beyond adenotonsillectomy are generally reserved for children with comorbidities causing multilevel airway obstruction. Persistent OSA following adenotonsillectomy may result from hypertrophy of the nasal turbinates, deviated nasal septum, adenoidal regrowth, lingual tonsil hypertrophy, or enlargement of the tongue base. Targeted surgical interventions addressing these specific anatomic abnormalities are required to relieve upper airway obstruction and resolve persistent OSA in affected children.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26809.r32">[32]</xref></p>
        <p>In pediatric patients&#x000a0;with complex comorbidities,&#x000a0;such as obesity, Down syndrome, craniofacial abnormalities,&#x000a0;or neuromuscular disorders, positive airway pressure (PAP) therapy&#x000a0;may be implemented using CPAP, bilevel PAP, or invasive ventilation. Tracheotomy may be necessary in severe cases.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26809.r33">[33]</xref></p>
        <p>Tracheotomy is the definitive surgical treatment for OSA. Most patients requiring tracheotomy have severe craniofacial anomalies or neuromuscular hypotonia. Although some children are tracheostomy-dependent, the procedure may also serve as a temporary intervention to manage severe OSA while awaiting additional surgical management.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26809.r34">[34]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-26809.s9" sec-type="Differential Diagnosis">
        <title>Differential Diagnosis</title>
        <p>Pediatric OSA shares overlapping symptoms with multiple conditions. Accurate diagnosis and differentiation of OSA from these potential mimics require PSG.</p>
        <p>Nasal congestion&#x000a0;from allergic&#x000a0;rhinitis often&#x000a0;contributes to&#x000a0;snoring and should&#x000a0;be managed accordingly. Behavioral manifestations such as hyperactivity or inattention can mimic attention-deficit/hyperactivity disorder, while learning difficulties may be mistaken for developmental delay. Nocturnal gastroesophageal reflux can produce brief breathing pauses and may exacerbate adenotonsillar hypertrophy, increasing OSA risk. Secondary nocturnal enuresis warrants evaluation for OSA.&#x000a0;Morning headaches, often due to overnight carbon dioxide retention, require assessment for atypical features. Parasomnias may be increased but are not specific, and daytime sleepiness in older children should prompt consideration of narcolepsy.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-26809.s10" sec-type="Prognosis">
        <title>Prognosis</title>
        <p>Timely management of modifiable risk factors is essential to mitigate the long-term consequences of pediatric OSA. Interventions include weight management in children with&#x000a0;overweight, optimal control of allergic rhinitis, and reduction of exposure to secondhand smoke. Adenotonsillectomy is the 1st-line treatment, while&#x000a0;CPAP therapy may be appropriate&#x000a0;for children who experience residual OSA or cannot undergo surgery. Untreated or persistent OSA can impair attention, learning, memory, quality of life, and emotional well-being.&#x000a0;The condition also increases the risk of chronic conditions, such as hypertension and diabetes, and, in severe cases, may result in growth impairment and failure to thrive.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26809.r35">[35]</xref></p>
        <p>Children at higher risk for OSA are also at increased risk of neurocognitive deficits later in life if the condition is unaddressed. Prompt identification and management can prevent long-term complications and preserve overall neurodevelopmental and physiological health.</p>
        <p>After adenotonsillectomy, a period of up to&#x000a0;6 months is generally appropriate to allow inflammation to resolve and sleep patterns to normalize. Patients should be reassessed at that time for improvements in sleep quality and function. Routine postoperative PSG is usually unnecessary.&#x000a0;Success&#x000a0;may&#x000a0;be evaluated based on improvements in initial behavioral or cognitive issues and reduced parental concern regarding observed sleep disturbances. If performed, postoperative PSG&#x000a0;can provide objective data on AHI&#x000a0;reductions. A comparative study of adenotonsillectomy versus watchful waiting demonstrated significant improvements in OSA symptoms, AHI, and behavior among surgical patients, although executive function and attention scores did not differ between groups.</p>
        <p>The majority of children experience symptomatic improvement following adenotonsillectomy. Data indicate superior outcomes compared to watchful waiting, with normalization of PSG findings in 79% of surgical patients versus 46% with conservative management. In children with mild OSA (AHI &#x0003e;1), symptoms persist in 19% to 73% of cases.&#x000a0;In severe OSA (AHI &#x0003e;5), persistent symptoms, including snoring and disrupted sleep, occur in only 13% to 29% of patients. Most children do not require long-term monitoring unless symptoms persist. Adenotonsillectomy is less effective in patients with Down syndrome, craniofacial abnormalities, or both.&#x000a0;These children require postoperative surveillance and at least annual repeat PSG due to a high risk of persistent or recurrent OSA. Children with obesity also require weight management to optimize postoperative outcomes.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26809.r36">[36]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-26809.s11" sec-type="Complications">
        <title>Complications</title>
        <p>If left untreated, pediatric OSA&#x000a0;may result in significant morbidity and long-term complications. Sustained hypoxia increases pulmonary vasoconstriction, which&#x000a0;may lead to pulmonary hypertension and right heart failure at an early age. Cognitive dysfunction, impaired learning, and poor school performance are frequently associated with undiagnosed or untreated pediatric OSA. Additionally, the increased work of breathing may contribute to failure to thrive, particularly in younger children.</p>
        <p>Surgical intervention must be approached with caution, as adenotonsillectomy, while effective, is not without risk. Adverse events following tonsillectomy are uncommon but can occur. The most frequent postoperative complications include localized pain, reduced oral intake, and dehydration during the acute recovery period. More serious complications include bleeding, secondary infection, respiratory compromise, velopharyngeal insufficiency, and subglottic stenosis.&#x000a0;As with any procedure involving airway management, patients may develop bronchospasm or laryngospasm.&#x000a0;Furthermore, the severity of OSA is predictive of postoperative risk.&#x000a0;Children with more severe disease may require prolonged observation in the postanesthesia care unit or inpatient monitoring following surgery.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-26809.s12" sec-type="Deterrence and Patient Education">
        <title>Deterrence and Patient Education</title>
        <p>Before diagnosis, parents should be educated during well-child visits to remain vigilant for signs and symptoms of pediatric OSA, including loud nightly snoring, frequent nighttime awakenings, secondary nocturnal enuresis, and behavioral changes. Additionally, caregivers of&#x000a0;children with elevated body weight or a family history of obesity should receive guidance regarding&#x000a0;the complications of adiposity, including&#x000a0;the heightened risk of pediatric OSA.</p>
        <p>Healthcare providers must emphasize the importance of recognizing sleep disturbances, as caregivers may not always perceive dysfunctional sleep patterns. An additional component of patient and caregiver education involves setting appropriate expectations regarding outcomes following adenotonsillectomy.&#x000a0;Complete symptom resolution may not occur in children with obesity without concurrent weight reduction.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-26809.s13" sec-type="Pearls and Other Issues">
        <title>Pearls and Other Issues</title>
        <p>Children presenting with loud nightly snoring, fragmented sleep, and behavioral disturbances should be screened for OSA. A subset of patients may exhibit additional symptoms, including nocturnal enuresis, morning headaches, irritability, and declining academic performance. Maintaining a high index of suspicion and obtaining a detailed sleep history are essential to identify OSA in these cases.</p>
        <p>Furthermore, all children being evaluated for attention-deficit/hyperactivity disorder should be assessed for sleep quality and duration before the initiation of pharmacologic therapy. Early recognition and management of OSA can significantly improve academic performance, behavior, and long-term health outcomes. Given the rising prevalence of pediatric obesity, OSA, an important comorbidity of&#x000a0;excessive adiposity, should be discussed routinely with caregivers during well-child visits.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-26809.s14" sec-type="Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes ">
        <title>Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes </title>
        <p>Prompt assessment, evaluation, diagnosis, and management are essential when pediatric OSA is suspected. Standardized screening practices, including a detailed sleep history, should be incorporated into every well-child examination.</p>
        <p>Effective management requires an interprofessional approach to prevent long-term complications. Optimal outcomes depend on coordination among the primary care provider, sleep study center, sleep physician, anesthesiologist, surgical team, and caregivers. Collaborative care ensures timely diagnosis and appropriate intervention.</p>
        <p>Healthcare providers must emphasize education regarding sleep health to facilitate communication among caregivers and specialists when pediatric OSA is suspected or confirmed. Continued research is needed to refine screening tools, optimize management strategies, and strengthen provider education. These goals demand a coordinated effort among researchers, subspecialists, and primary care physicians.</p>
        <p>Obesity has emerged as a major risk factor for pediatric OSA, particularly in adolescents. Prevention and management of obesity are therefore critical components in reducing the incidence and long-term burden of OSA in this population.</p>
        <p>Recognition of pediatric OSA often begins with parental awareness of symptoms and discussion with the primary care physician. The primary care physician plays a central role in the initial assessment, focusing on symptom evaluation and management of contributing factors such as nasal congestion and seasonal allergies. Optimal control of these conditions can reduce upper airway obstruction and improve sleep quality.</p>
        <p>Referral to an allergy and immunology specialist may be warranted in cases of moderate-to-severe allergic symptoms. Based on the persistence or severity of symptoms and the response to supportive measures, patients should be referred for PSG under the care of a pediatric pulmonologist.</p>
        <p>The sleep study findings and recommendations from the sleep medicine or pulmonary specialist guide subsequent management. If indicated, referral to an otolaryngologist for consideration of adenotonsillectomy is appropriate. Preoperative evaluation by the anesthesiologist and operating room team ensures patient safety and perioperative readiness.&#x000a0;Supportive measures should be maintained postoperatively.&#x000a0;Follow-up with sleep medicine or pulmonary specialists is necessary to reassess OSA resolution through repeat PSG.</p>
        <p>Obesity is an additional contributing factor, particularly in older children. Ongoing management emphasizing healthy weight reduction should involve collaboration with nutritionists, dietitians, and pediatric obesity specialists to optimize long-term outcomes.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-26809.s15">
        <title>Review Questions</title>
        <list list-type="bullet">
