      <floats-group id="article-26809.s16">
        <fig id="article-26809.image.f1" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Etiology of Pediatric Obstructive Sleep Apnea.&#x000a0;This image shows that pediatric obstructive sleep apnea&#x000a0;arises&#x000a0;from a combination of factors that narrow or compromise the upper airway. Enlarged tonsils and adenoids or craniofacial issues often&#x000a0;lead to anatomical obstructions. Physiological causes stem from genetic or neuromuscular issues that reduce airway tone and integrity. Environmental triggers, including allergic rhinitis, chronic sinusitis, and exposure to pollutants, exacerbate nasal congestion, contributing to the blockage. Contributed by SJ Kadam, MD</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="Etiology__of__Pediatric__Obstructive__Sleep__Apnea" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-26809.s17">
        <fig id="article-26809.image.f2" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Anatomical Differences in Pediatric Airway Related to Sleep Apnea.&#x000a0;The image compares normal airway anatomy with typical factors causing sleep apnea, such as large tonsils and swollen adenoids. Could your child have obstructive sleep apnea? Home Sleep Web site. www.https://www.homesleepllc.com/blog/could-your-child-have-obstructive-sleep-apnea. Published&#x000a0;May 24, 2018. Accessed&#x000a0;January 15, 2026.</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="Pediatric__OSA" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-26809.s18">
        <fig id="article-26809.image.f3" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Pathologic Breathing Cycle in Obstructive Sleep Apnea.&#x000a0;The cycle initiates with the onset of sleep, which reduces airway stability and leads to airway collapse. The resulting drop in oxygen and rise in carbon dioxide stimulate increased breathing effort and a "fight or flight" stress response. This acute stress causes arousal and disturbed sleep, followed by hyperventilation upon waking. The chronic result of this cycle is intermittent hypoxia, sleep fragmentation, and impaired glucose metabolism. Contributed by SJ Kadam, MD</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="Workflow__Diagram__Whiteboard____x3x" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-26809.s19">
        <fig id="article-26809.image.f4" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Evaluation of Pediatric Obstructive Sleep Apnea.&#x000a0;The clinical evaluation of pediatric obstructive sleep apnea relies on a 3-part approach.&#x000a0;The history explores common and uncommon symptoms, associated comorbidities, and responses to screening questionnaires. Physical examination emphasizes recognition of adenoid facies and dysmorphic features, in addition to cardiac and neurologic assessment. Diagnostic confirmation relies on polysomnography, supported by other modalities, including imaging and sleep endoscopy, as indicated. Contributed by SJ Kadam, MD</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="Evaluation__of__Pediatric__Obstructive__Sleep__Apnea__xOSAx" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
